{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 67, :comments ["<b>And Yitzhak brought her into the tent of Sarah his mother.</b> First it mentioned her by pronoun, when it said ויביאה ('and he brought her'), and afterward it mentioned her by name, when it said ויקח את רבקה ('and he took Rivka'). And the verse should properly have said, 'And Yitzhak took Rivka and brought her into the tent of Sarah his mother'; but it is possible to interpret, by way of the secret of the matter, that Scripture intended to give Sarah precedence over Rivka, as is fitting, because she preceded her — and Rivka was not born until after Sarah died. Therefore Scripture says (Genesis 22:22–23): 'ובתואל ילד את רבקה,' and immediately after it: 'ותמת שרה' — and its meaning is that Sarah had already died at the Akedah. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that Yitzhak was thirty-seven years old at the time of the Akedah, and when Sarah heard of the Akedah her soul flew forth and she died. And Scripture testifies: 'ויהי יצחק בן מ׳ שנה בקחתו את רבקה' — hence she was three years old when he took her." "<b>And from this</b> the verse comes out correctly, that it mentioned ויביאה by pronoun, and Scripture says: ויבאה יצחק האהלה שרה אמו ויקח את רבקה. For Yitzhak brought her into the tent of Sarah his mother when he took Rivka, and therefore it says ויביאה — because the primordial love was aroused. And understand well that to this our Rabbis of blessed memory alluded in the Midrash there, where they said: 'Sarah his mother — and she was Sarah his mother.' For all the years that Sarah was alive, a lamp would burn from Sabbath eve to Sabbath eve, and a blessing was found in the dough, and a cloud was attached over the tent; and from the time Sarah died they ceased, and when Rivka came they returned." "<b>And Yitzhak was comforted after his mother.</b> Scripture declares the virtue of Yitzhak, that he would not accept consolation for his mother until Rivka came and he was comforted through her." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'And Yitzhak brought her into the tent of Sarah his mother' — from this you learn that if a man's wife dies and he has sons from her, he should not marry another until he marries off his sons first, and afterward he may take a wife for himself; for it says: ויביאה יצחק האהלה, and after it is written: ויוסף אברהם ויקח אשה."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:59.439269920Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}